# machine only
#
# A program that writes one of its own instructions. It reads an operation
# code (1 +, 2 ·, 3 ∸, 4 :, 6 ÷, 7 mod, 8 ∪, 9 ∩) and two numbers, builds the
# instruction number for "a op b → c", and puts it where group P begins.
#
# An instruction is fourteen digits: first operand, operation, second
# operand, result. a, b and c live at addresses 2, 3 and 4, so the number is
#     2·10^10 + o·10^8 + 3·10^4 + 4
#
# This only means something on the machine. Read as algebra, the formulas of
# a program are not numbers and cannot be overwritten.

π' → A
? → o
? → a
? → b
2·10000000000 + o·100000000 + 30004 → ↓P
P → π

π' → P
Ω → Ω               # replaced before it is reached
c → ?
Ω → π
